---
/**
 * /about — About SOURDEN  (About brief §01–§18)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * This route used to render a reservation page. It is now the real thing: the
 * BRAND POSITIONING AND TRUST page — what Sourden is, why it exists, what
 * problem it is built around, who it is for, and what it will not claim.
 *
 * STRUCTURE (brief)
 *   01 Hero                        §01  eyebrow, H1, copy, tagline, two actions
 *   02 Why Sourden exists          §02  the ecosystem, and who it is hard for
 *   03 The gap                     §03  buying directly / large org / Sourden
 *   04 What Sourden believes       §04  the statement, then the argument
 *   05 Sourcing without barriers   §05  INDIVIDUAL → … → SCALE
 *   06 Source at your scale        §06  five stages, and the MOQ position
 *   07 Who we are built for        §07  five groups, hand-off to /industries
 *   08 What we actually do         §08  requirement → … → destination (ink)
 *   09 What we don't claim         §09  four limits, stated plainly
 *   10 How we work                 §10  six stages, numbered, link to process
 *   11 The Sourden standard        §11  four principles
 *   12 China is not one supply     §12  the supplier landscape is regional
 *      chain
 *   13 Experience without the show §13  the promise, after the description
 *   14 What makes a good partner   §14  buyer ↔ Sourden ↔ supplier
 *   15 Our services                §15  the five services, hand-off to /services
 *   16 About the business          §16  what the business does, no numbers
 *   17 FAQ                         §17  eight questions
 *   18 Final CTA                   §18  one primary action
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
 *   · It does not repeat `/services`. §15 gives each service one line and hands
 *     the reader on; the five descriptions in `services.js` stay on the pages
 *     that explain them, and the §15 sentences are a different, shorter set.
 *   · It does not repeat `/how-it-works`. §10 names the six stages in five
 *     words each and links there; the page that explains what happens inside
 *     each stage is still the process page.
 *   · It does not attack anyone. §03 puts three ways of working side by side
 *     with the same weight and no ranking, because the brief forbids
 *     criticising sourcing agents, procurement companies and competitors.
 *   · It does not present Sourden as a manufacturer. §08 is a chain Sourden
 *     coordinates, and the diagram runs from the customer's requirement to the
 *     customer's destination with Sourden as one link in it.
 *   · It does not invent a company fact. No founding year, founder, team size,
 *     office, country count, order volume, customer number, revenue,
 *     certification, award, partnership, client logo, testimonial or case
 *     study appears on it — the brief forbids all of them by name, and §16
 *     describes the business through what it does instead.
 *   · It does not redesign anything. Header, footer, buttons, rows, media, the
 *     hero, the check-list shape, the review grid, the rail and the FAQ are all
 *     the existing site's. Only two devices are new, and both are one page's
 *     composition of existing elements (`.ab-statement`, `.ab-flow`).
 *
 * INDEXABILITY
 *   This page is real, so it is indexable and appears in sitemap.xml. Its entry
 *   in `reservedTopLevel` (src/data/routes.js) was removed in the same change —
 *   that list is what drives the noindex/sitemap split, so forgetting it would
 *   have left a finished, content-rich page excluded from search.
 *
 * STRUCTURED DATA
 *   WebPage + BreadcrumbList + one Service node per service §15 lists, built
 *   from the same records the section renders, so a structured-data claim can
 *   never describe a service the page does not show.
 *
 *   No FAQPage node: the brief asks for FAQ structured data "if supported by
 *   the existing project", and it is not — `schema.js` has no FAQ builder and
 *   no page emits one. Adding it here alone would have made `/about` the only
 *   page on the site with it, so it is left as a deliberate follow-up (add the
 *   builder to `schema.js`, apply it to all four FAQ-bearing sections at once).
 *
 * IMPORT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING
 *   Layout first, then the shared sections, then the detail-page sections, then
 *   this page's own sheet — otherwise these rules lose every same-specificity
 *   conflict against the shared core. `npm run audit` fails the build if that
 *   regresses.
 *
 *   This page imports `services-shared.css`, `service-detail.css` AND
 *   `how-it-works.css`, because it composes devices those sheets own (the hero
 *   and FAQ; the editorial prose and review grid; the stages, growth and rows
 *   lists). The file names say "services" and "how-it-works" because those
 *   pages were each device's first consumer; the devices themselves are generic
 *   editorial sections, and the brief asks for exactly this — reuse, and no
 *   second visual language. See `src/styles/index.css` → "SHARING A DEVICE
 *   BETWEEN TWO PAGES", which now lists four pages sharing these two sheets.
 *
 * WHY A SECTION DISPATCHER
 *   The brief numbers eighteen sections and gives each one's copy verbatim. A
 *   dispatcher means the page's order, tones and content live in the data file
 *   and nothing about the composition is buried in markup — the same
 *   architecture `/services`, the five detail pages, `/industries` and
 *   `/how-it-works` use, so a section can be reordered or inserted without
 *   touching a template. An unknown `type` throws at build time rather than
 *   silently dropping a section.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/services-shared.css';
import '../styles/service-detail.css';
import '../styles/how-it-works.css';
import '../styles/about.css';

import AboutFlow from '../components/about/AboutFlow.astro';
import AboutStatement from '../components/about/AboutStatement.astro';
import FinalCTA from '../components/FinalCTA.astro';
import HiGrowth from '../components/how-it-works/HiGrowth.astro';
import HiLinkRows from '../components/how-it-works/HiLinkRows.astro';
import HiStages from '../components/how-it-works/HiStages.astro';
import ServiceProse from '../components/services/ServiceProse.astro';
import ServiceReviewGrid from '../components/services/ServiceReviewGrid.astro';
import ServicesFaq from '../components/services/ServicesFaq.astro';
import ServicesHero from '../components/services/ServicesHero.astro';

import {
  breadcrumbs,
  faqFoot,
  faqIntro,
  faqItems,
  finalCta,
  hero,
  meta,
  relatedServices,
  sections,
} from '../data/about-page.js';
import { breadcrumbItems, breadcrumbSchema, faqPageSchema, serviceSchema, webPageSchema } from '../data/schema.js';

const path = '/about';

/**
 * One graph for the page: the page itself, its breadcrumb, and a Service node
 * for each of the five services §15 lists.
 *
 * `serviceSchema` is the site's existing builder and emits no price, rating or
 * availability — none of those can be evidenced. `/services`, `/industries` and
 * `/how-it-works` declare the same five nodes the same way.
 */
const schema = [
  webPageSchema({ title: meta.title, description: meta.description, path }),
  breadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbItems(breadcrumbs)),
  ...relatedServices.map((service) =>
    serviceSchema({ name: service.title, description: service.description, href: service.href })
  ),
  /* Built from the same `faqItems` <ServicesFaq> renders below, so the node can
     never describe a question a visitor cannot see. */
  faqPageSchema(faqItems),
];

/**
 * Every section needs a unique id for `aria-labelledby`.
 *
 * Derived from the index rather than written by hand, so inserting a section
 * cannot produce a duplicate id. Numbered from `index + 2` because the array
 * starts at the brief's §02 — §01 is the hero, which owns its own id — so the
 * ids line up with the brief's numbering and a reader can go straight from
 * "§07" in the brief to `id="ab-07"` in the build.
 */
const sectionId = (index) => `ab-${String(index + 2).padStart(2, '0')}`;
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <ServicesHero
    breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
    eyebrow={hero.eyebrow}
    title={hero.title}
    description={hero.description}
    tagline={hero.tagline}
    primaryCta={hero.primaryCta}
    secondaryCta={hero.secondaryCta}
    imageKey={hero.image.key}
    imageCaption={hero.image.caption}
    titleId="ab-01"
  />

  {
    sections.map((section, index) => {
      const titleId = sectionId(index);

      switch (section.type) {
        case 'prose':
          /* §02, §12, §16. The shared editorial prose device: heading on the
             left, paragraphs on the right, an optional brass closing line. */
          return (
            <ServiceProse
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              paragraphs={section.paragraphs}
              emphasis={section.emphasis}
              foot={section.foot}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={titleId}
            />
          );

        case 'statement':
          /* §04, §13. The same grid, with the section's one display line —
             before the copy in §04, after it in §13. The position comes from
             the data, because the two briefs put the sentence in different
             places. */
          return (
            <AboutStatement
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              statement={section.statement}
              statementPosition={section.statementPosition}
              paragraphs={section.paragraphs}
              foot={section.foot}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={titleId}
            />
          );

        case 'reviewGrid':
          /* §03, §07, §11. N titled items with a sentence each, as a two-column
             editorial list. §07 is the one that also carries a closing link. */
          return (
            <ServiceReviewGrid
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              description={section.description}
              items={section.items}
              note={section.note}
              foot={section.foot}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={titleId}
            />
          );

        case 'stages':
          /* §06 (five, unnumbered) and §10 (six, numbered). The difference
             between the two is a data shape, not a layout — §10 is a sequence
             with a first step, §06 is where a buyer currently is. */
          return (
            <HiStages
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              description={section.description}
              paragraphs={section.paragraphs}
              items={section.items}
              numbered={section.numbered}
              note={section.note}
              foot={section.foot}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={titleId}
            />
          );

        case 'growth':
          /* §05. Copy above the progression, copy below it — the second block
             is why `HiGrowth` learned `closingParagraphs`. */
          return (
            <HiGrowth
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              paragraphs={section.paragraphs}
              progression={section.progression}
              closingParagraphs={section.closingParagraphs}
              emphasis={section.emphasis}
              foot={section.foot}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={titleId}
            />
          );

        case 'flow':
          /* §08 (vertical, on the ink band) and §14 (horizontal). Same device,
             two directions — see the component for why the direction is the
             brief's decision rather than a layout preference. */
          return (
            <AboutFlow
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              paragraphs={section.paragraphs}
              orientation={section.orientation}
              nodes={section.nodes}
              marker={section.marker}
              note={section.note}
              foot={section.foot}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={titleId}
            />
          );

        case 'linkRows':
          /* §09 (four statements, no destinations) and §15 (the five services,
             numbered, one destination each). The rows device takes both: what
             differs is whether a row carries a number and whether it is itself
             the link.
             `note` is what carries §09's closing line ("Clear information is
             more useful than false certainty."). It is passed explicitly
             rather than spread, because the device's prop list is the contract
             — a field the component does not accept would be dropped silently,
             and that is exactly how this sentence went missing the first
             time. */
          return (
            <HiLinkRows
              eyebrow={section.eyebrow}
              title={section.title}
              description={section.description}
              items={section.items}
              numbered={section.numbered}
              note={section.note}
              foot={section.foot}
              tone={section.tone}
              titleId={titleId}
            />
          );

        default:
          /* A typo in a `type` string must not render nothing and pass the
             build — the section would simply vanish from the page. */
          throw new Error(
            `[/about] Unknown section type "${section.type}" at index ${index}. ` +
              `See the section vocabulary in src/data/about-page.js.`
          );
      }
    })
  }

  <ServicesFaq
    eyebrow={faqIntro.eyebrow}
    title={faqIntro.title}
    items={faqItems}
    foot={faqFoot}
    titleId="ab-17"
  />

  <FinalCTA
    eyebrow={finalCta.eyebrow}
    title={finalCta.title}
    description={finalCta.description}
    cta={finalCta.cta}
  />
</BaseLayout>
